WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:07.280
I’m Clarice Smith, and I sing and dance in a black vaudeville show.

2
00:00:07.281 --> 00:00:16.057
Vaudeville is the name performers have given to the many traveling shows that move from town to town throughout America.

3
00:00:16.058 --> 00:00:26.788
Vaudeville shows can have singers, dancers, musicians, jugglers, and magicians and they play in big cities and small towns.

4
00:00:26.789 --> 00:00:30.665
Some vaudeville people even perform in tents!

5
00:00:30.666 --> 00:00:36.888
They rent out a farmer’s field, put up a tent, and use oil lamps for stage lights.

6
00:00:36.889 --> 00:00:47.716
I’ve been in vaudeville since 1900, when I was 16, and I have performed in big shows and small shows in a thousand different places.

7
00:00:47.717 --> 00:00:57.651
Sometimes, when my husband Charlie and I are performing with a big show with lots of stars, we travel in our own railroad car.

8
00:00:57.652 --> 00:01:07.417
But we’ve been in small shows too, with just a piano player and a comedian, and then we drive from place to place in an old car.

9
00:01:07.418 --> 00:01:14.393
Growing up, I’d watch boys sing and dance on the street corner, and people would throw them pennies.

10
00:01:14.394 --> 00:01:19.357
It gave me the idea that I could make some money the same way.

11
00:01:19.358 --> 00:01:26.552
We were poor and nobody took dance or music lessons, so I learned songs from the boys.

12
00:01:26.553 --> 00:01:31.384
I’d just sing and move my feet any way that I liked.

13
00:01:31.385 --> 00:01:41.734
People enjoyed my singing so much they threw me nickels, which I gave to my mama since she didn’t make much money washing people’s clothes.

14
00:01:41.735 --> 00:01:48.654
My lucky break came when one of the most popular shows in the South came to my little town.

15
00:01:48.655 --> 00:01:54.159
The show was Crackling Charlie and the Butterbeans, and they were wonderful.

16
00:01:54.160 --> 00:02:00.135
The Butterbeans were the best band I’d ever seen, and they could really get people dancing.

17
00:02:00.136 --> 00:02:03.522
I was 13 the first time I saw Charlie.

18
00:02:03.523 --> 00:02:10.876
He was the leader of the band and he stopped to see me dance and sing on the street. He gave me a dollar!

19
00:02:10.877 --> 00:02:18.582
The next time he came to town I was 14, and he gave me another dollar and a red ribbon.

20
00:02:18.583 --> 00:02:26.315
Two years later Charlie came to my house and asked Mama if I could join his show and marry him too.

21
00:02:26.316 --> 00:02:31.271
I knew that joining the show would be much better than doing laundry like Mama,

22
00:02:31.272 --> 00:02:36.891
so I told her that I was going with Charlie whether she agreed to let me go or not.

23
00:02:36.892 --> 00:02:46.185
Mama said yes, so Charlie and I got married. Suddenly I was in show business!

24
00:02:46.186 --> 00:02:54.202
Right away the piano player taught me some new songs and showed me how to sing them in a way that made people take notice.

25
00:02:54.203 --> 00:03:03.256
I soon realized that performing on stage was a lot different than singing on the street, so I paid close attention to my costumes.

26
00:03:03.257 --> 00:03:12.253
I sewed beads on a headband that I wore onstage, so that when the lights flashed on those beads, I sparkled!

27
00:03:12.254 --> 00:03:20.100
I sewed fringe on some of my dresses too, and I looked so wonderful, people couldn’t stop clapping when I sang.

28
00:03:20.101 --> 00:03:28.068
But performing is hard work too, because in vaudeville you need to be a great entertainer and develop different talents.

29
00:03:28.069 --> 00:03:31.696
You have to learn new things and practice all the time.

30
00:03:31.697 --> 00:03:40.595
For example, it took Charlie and me a whole week to learn a new kind of tap dancing called the soft shoe.

31
00:03:40.596 --> 00:03:50.048
It was a popular dance that everybody wanted to see, so all the vaudeville performers started putting that dance in their shows.

32
00:03:50.049 --> 00:03:58.112
Taps are small pieces of metal on the bottoms of your shoes that make a nice click-click-click when you dance.

33
00:03:58.113 --> 00:04:05.150
For the soft shoe, we took the taps off and danced quietly on sand that we’d put on the floor ahead of time.

34
00:04:05.151 --> 00:04:11.603
Our feet made a nice swishing sound, and the audience loved it!

35
00:04:11.604 --> 00:04:20.426
Since it’s against the law for whites and blacks to work or socialize together, there are separate theaters for white and black people.

36
00:04:20.427 --> 00:04:25.319
Charlie and I have performed at many famous black theaters in the country,

37
00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:28.831
such as the Douglass Theater in Macon, Georgia;

38
00:04:28.832 --> 00:04:35.417
the Liberty in Chattanooga, Tennessee; and even Gibson’s Standard Theater in Philadelphia.

39
00:04:35.418 --> 00:04:40.960
Sometimes we’re in town for a week and sometimes we stay only a day.

40
00:04:40.961 --> 00:04:49.020
But I always pack enough food to take with us when we’re on the road, because black people aren’t allowed to eat in most restaurants.

41
00:04:49.021 --> 00:04:57.361
The local hotels won’t let us rent rooms either, so whenever we get to a town, we usually stay at someone’s home.

42
00:04:57.362 --> 00:05:03.702
It costs thirty cents to see our show, which is a good price for such a lot of entertainment.

43
00:05:03.703 --> 00:05:12.710
Of course the performers only make about $30 a week each, because the theater owner usually takes most of admission price.

44
00:05:12.711 --> 00:05:17.298
Right now our show has a full band and a magician too.

45
00:05:17.299 --> 00:05:22.561
Most of the time, we do four performances a day, seven days a week.

46
00:05:22.562 --> 00:05:27.834
Recently Charlie and I have been doing a new comedy routine.

47
00:05:27.835 --> 00:05:32.299
We come out on stage dancing, and we look happy.

48
00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:37.743
Then I stop dancing and say, "Charlie, you talk too much.

49
00:05:37.744 --> 00:05:41.221
Sometimes I wish you were like a town clock."

50
00:05:41.222 --> 00:05:43.691
"Why, honey?" he asks.

51
00:05:43.692 --> 00:05:48.389
"Because then you’d only speak once an hour," I answer.

52
00:05:48.390 --> 00:05:51.325
The audience always laughs at that.

53
00:05:51.326 --> 00:05:54.316
Charlie and I do a few more dance steps.

54
00:05:54.317 --> 00:06:00.248
Then I stop and say, "Charlie, what’s the difference between a teacher and an engineer?"

55
00:06:00.249 --> 00:06:04.393
"I don’t know, honey. What is the difference?" he asks.

56
00:06:04.394 --> 00:06:09.097
"One trains the mind and the other minds the train," I answer.

57
00:06:09.098 --> 00:06:14.567
The audience loves to see a married couple tell jokes.

58
00:06:14.568 --> 00:06:23.465
Sometimes Charlie asks me if I want to settle down and have children, and he talks about getting a little place with enough land to farm.

59
00:06:23.466 --> 00:06:32.508
But times are hard and we don’t have enough money saved right now, so I want to keep on singing and dancing as long as I can.

60
00:06:32.509 --> 00:06:38.304
If you ever want to have a good time, look for Crackling Charlie and his Clarice.

61
00:06:38.305 --> 00:06:48.438
We’ll be looking for you, and the Butterbeans will be right behind us playing their hearts out.

